  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   This solution extends the chained optimization approach from the parent by incorporating four key enhancements to target the improvement of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem constant C:\n   \n   \u2022 Progressive resolution optimization: Iteratively increasing resolution to allow larger-scale features to emerge, particularly at critical points where overlaps are suspected to be maximized.\n   \u2022 Frequency-domain smoothing: Applying least squares optimization to correlation spectral gaps to directly penalize worst-case overlaps rather than smoothing shifted copies.\n   \n   The core bottleneck addressed is the efficient targeting of high-overlap regions at large shifts through multi-scale optimization and spectrum-based features. We implement rigorous convex penalization of the worst-case shift while retaining the problem's sparsity through \u03b2-constrained frequency projections.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   The optimization targets: min \u2225y_k\u2225_\u221e for k\u2208{-n+1,...,n-1}, subject to:\n   \u2022 x_j \u2208 [2j/n, 2(j+1)/n] for all j (discretization)\n   \u2022 \u2211x_j = 1 (integral constraint)\n   \u2022 Time-domain to frequency conversion:\n     A = FFT {1 - \u03b4_k} for k=0,...,2n-2 (shift vectors)\n     B = FFT {x}\n\n   Improved objective: \n      min_{\u03b2>0} max_k { \u2225A_inv * (B * \u03b4_t) * (I - R_\u03b2)\u03b4_k\u2225 }(1)\n   where R_\u03b2(\u03c9) = exp(-\u03b2|\u03c9|) is a reddening operator that penalizes high-frequency sharp overlaps.\n\n   For feasibility, we enforce via linear transform:\n      D = diag(I - diag(|F|>1))\n      D_x = diag(floor(project_{L1} D_x^T s))\n\n   Each solution is verified using:\n      verify_integral = sum(h)\n      verify_bounds = all(0\u2264h[i]\u22641)\n      verify_correlation = max_k(x)compute_c5(h,k)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Phase1: Progressive multi-resolution initialization (400,600,800)  \n   - All candidates start from parent's solution projected to resolution\n   - Bounds enforced via clipping followed by mass rebalancing with Gaussian weighting\n   \n   Phase2: Spectral smoothing (iteratively)  \n   - Compute reduced correlation spectrum: C\u0303 = FFT-inverse( FFT(C) * (1 - exp(-\u03b2|\u03c9|)) )\n   - Solve via projective gradient descent with:\n        x \u2190 proj_{[0,1]} (x - \u03b7\u2207f(x))\n        where f(x)=max_k compute_c5(x,k)\n        gradient = sum_k \u2202c_5/\u2202x = [cumulative sum(consine shifts)]\n\n   Phase3: Alternating-frequency optimization (with warm starts every 500 iterations)\n   - Penalize absolute deviations via:\n        L = R(x) + \u03b3 * ||x||_1\n        where R(x) = max_k (compute_c5(x,k))\n   - Adapts \u03b3 starting from 0.005 to 0.1 based on estimated correlation width\n\n   Phase4: Constrained optimization pipeline\n   1. Greedy binary initial (with resolutions of 100, 400, 800 steps)\n   2. Chebyshev projection to [0,1]\n   3. Coordinate-wise descent with randomized lazy updates\n   4. Frequency spectral gap minimization (principal component analysis on weight space)\n   5. Noise injection during local minima detection\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n   \u2022 Three resolution configurations automated: n=400,600,800\n   \u2022 Accelerated optimization: \n        - Use n_choices=[400,800] in Progressive phase\n        - Perform frequency annealing from \u03b2=0.1->0.01\n   \u2022 Warm-start triggers at:\n        - 250 iterations\n        - 75% budget consumed\n        - 3 failed gradient attempts in a row\n   \u2022 Comparison function:\n        comparison = min_k(n_k) * (1 + small_shift_term)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - time_estimate(n): 0.2*n + 0.005*n^2 seconds\n   - Deadline checks written at beginning of each pipeline step\n   - Feasibility restoration uses Gibbs sampling with clipping if >5 consecutive infeasible updates\n   - Fallback: if iteration exceeds degree_bound=0, return parent's solution\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   Each candidate is validated via:\n   - 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i (float tolerance=1e-6)\n   - Integral constraint: sum(h) - 2/(3*\u03c3) should be within 1e-5\n\n   Only projected solutions are evaluated. The final candidate:\n      must have n/2 * mean(h)\u22481\n      correlation score below 0.3808 threshold\n   Returns (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) formatted as numpy arrays.\n\n   The optimization procedure involves explicitly projecting onto feasible set via:\n        h = (h + h_min + h_max) / (h_min + h_max+1)\n        then clipping to [0,1] and adjusting to ensure integral=1\n        Enforcement is done by iterative scalar balancing with Metropolis acceptance",
